Durarara!! AU
Chapter 22 – Part 1
Curtis went home first.
The uniform came off like the day he was trying to shed: school doorway, ruined shoes, Russia Sushi, fortune-cookie math, a later hour that no longer belonged in Raira colors. He was not heading back into the street immediately. Anri had said she would come to him. That made the apartment the meeting place, and that made the look his problem instead of the city’s.
He opted for cleaner.
Black shirt. Red tie. White suspenders against white pants. Red Jordan cap pulled on over the long black braids. The mismatched eyes — one green, one blue — sat sharper in the mirror once the day had been wiped off him. It was not the masked night gear and not the compression-shirt exhaustion either. Just a cleaner evening version of himself, as if showing up to his own flat like a host instead of a boy who had spent the afternoon being a problem in three different rooms.
He checked the time. Anri was still later. The suite was quiet. The two duffle bags stayed where they always stayed: one he would spend, one he would not touch. He looked, for a moment, like someone prepared for company rather than collapse.

Across the ward, Celty finally read the messages all the way through.
its fine celty. we were both upset that night.
you can take back what you said about the soft landing and all that jazz.
i already forgave you.
i just didnt get a chance to tell you because mei is….well. something else.
The relief hit her so hard she had to sit with the helmet off and the PDA braced in both hands.
Safe. For now.
Not the other night, not exactly. That door was still a door. But the immediate threat on the table had been named and fenced. He had not given Mei an address. He had called the inquiry shameless in Simon’s shop. He had paid and left before the woman could turn dinner into a second appointment. Celty had taken his cookie. She had shut down the physical. And now the texts sat in her screen like a temporary ceasefire: he had forgiven her, Mei was something else, and he had not vanished into the city without answering.
Black smoke rose in a slower ribbon from the empty space above her collar, less battle-ready than it had been at the sushi counter.
Mei could not get him alone tonight.
That was enough to let Celty breathe.
She folded the two fortunes again — a lost object will be found and what you leave behind will follow you anyway — and for the first time since he had walked out of Russia Sushi, the second one felt a little less like a chase and a little more like a stay of execution.
On the other side of Ikebukuro, Curtis adjusted the red cap in his hallway mirror and waited for the knock that would make the cleaner look necessary.
Celty stared at the thread longer than the message required.
Then she typed something that was not a warning, not a fortune, and not a claim on his address.
Celty:
Thank you for answering.
And for saying you already forgave me.
A second slip followed, smaller, as if she had to talk herself into sending it.
Celty:
You were kinder in that text than the table deserved.
I hope the rest of your night is quieter than dinner was.
She looked at the last line, almost deleted it, then sent it anyway.

In the suite, Curtis’s phone lit against the black shirt and red tie. He glanced down, red cap tipping forward as the cleaner evening version of him read a message with no knives in it. Long black braids shifted against his shoulders.
For a second the sushi counter receded. No Mei. No specimen eyes. Just Celty, after the fact, saying thank you like the ceasefire mattered to her as much as the argument had.
He did not answer immediately. Anri was still on the way. The knock had not come yet. He stood in his own hallway in white suspenders and a red cap, thumb resting on a kind message from the woman who had taken his cookie and shut down his address, and let that be a quiet thing in a day that had produced almost none of them.
Curtis finally thumbed a reply, still standing in the hallway in the red cap and black shirt, the kinder tone of Celty’s message making the harder version of himself feel suddenly obvious.
Curtis:
thank you celty for everything so far.
im sorry im such a dick sometimes.
He sent it without dressing it up. No joke. No ninth-circle follow-up. Just the apology the sushi counter and the tower night had both earned.

Across the ward, Celty read it and actually chuckled to herself.
The sound had nowhere public to go. Helmet off, PDA in her hands, the two fortunes still folded nearby, she sat with the bluntness of im sorry im such a dick sometimes and felt the tension in her chest loosen another inch. That was him. Not the polite gift-bringer. Not the specimen. The boy who could shut Mei down like hell ice and then send a sentence that unvarnished a minute later.
Sometimes, she thought, almost fond. He says sometimes like it is a rare setting.
She did not text that part back. The chuckle was enough.

In the suite, Curtis’s phone lit again before he could pocket it.
Anri.
Anri:
I’m close.
Is it still okay to come?
A second message followed, smaller, as if she had walked the last block rehearsing it.
Anri:
I’m outside your building.
I can wait if you need a minute.
Curtis looked from Celty’s thread to Anri’s, red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes, the cleaner evening look suddenly serving its purpose. The later hour had arrived at the door. The quiet message from Celty was still warm in the palm under it.
Two conversations.
One hallway.
And a knock that was now only a flight of stairs away.
Curtis went outside.
The red cap caught the building light first, then the black shirt, the red tie, the white suspenders sharp against white pants. Long black braids hung down his chest as he stepped into the evening air looking less like the defeated boy from the school hallway and more like someone who had decided the later hour deserved a cleaner version of him.
Anri was already there, new Nikes on her feet, phone still in her hand from the last text. She looked up — and stopped.
“Hey, Anri-chan!”
The greeting was easy. Almost bright. Too ordinary for the day underneath it, which made it land harder.
Anri’s breath caught before she could hide it. The uniform was gone. The tired hallway slump was gone. In their place was this: mismatched eyes under a red Jordan cap, braids neat, the whole frame of him dressed like he had actually meant for her to come to the door. After the ruined pair, after I won’t do it again, after I can’t ignore you, the sight of him waiting outside his own building in that look made the claimed later hour suddenly, dangerously real.
“…Curtis-kun.” His name came out softer than she intended. Her eyes flicked over the tie and suspenders despite herself, then down to the new shoes he had bought and she had lectured him for, then back to his face. “You—you changed.”
A small, unsteady smile pulled at her mouth.
“Hi.”
She stepped closer, the Nikes quiet on the pavement, still holding the phone like she needed proof she had not imagined the invitation. The boy who had walked away from school looking defeated was standing in front of her now saying hey, Anri-chan as if the night might still be allowed to become simple.
Curtis held the building door, then the suite door, red cap tipping as he stepped aside.
“Come in.”
Anri crossed the threshold and stopped so abruptly the new Nikes made almost no sound.
The flat was not a first-year box.
Two bedrooms. Real furniture. A living room that looked chosen rather than scavenged. Warm light on clean surfaces, a couch that had already held too many versions of his night, a kitchen that belonged to someone who lived alone on purpose. Beyond that, the quiet evidence of money handled carefully instead of shown off: nothing gaudy, nothing temporary, just a place that had been paid into and then inhabited. A few still-packed boxes sat against one wall like the only reminder he had arrived from somewhere else. The rest of it felt settled. Too settled for the boy she had watched fall asleep in class and vanish into alleys.
“…This is yours?”
Curtis shut the door, long black braids shifting against the black shirt and white suspenders.
“Yeah. Home.”
Anri turned in a slow, stunned half-circle, the Nike box memory from the hallway colliding with the actual rooms those shoes had been walking toward. She had pictured something smaller. Dorm-adjacent. A transfer student’s temporary corner. Not this: space, quiet, a life already arranged behind a door only a few blocks from hers.
“It’s…” She searched for a word that would not sound like she was measuring his wallet and failed partway. “It’s really nice, Curtis-kun. I didn’t think—”
She caught herself, color touching her face, and looked at him instead of the rooms.
“I didn’t think you’d have a place like this. It feels like you actually live here. Not like you’re passing through.”
The later hour settled over the suite with that observation still in it. Anri stood in the middle of his flat in the shoes he had bought her, amazed by the home he had almost refused to let anyone follow him into, and suddenly very aware that after dinner had not been a street corner.
It had been this.
Curtis followed her look around the room and shrugged once, red cap tipping as if the suite’s first impression needed correcting before it turned into a story he had not meant to tell.
“It came furnished. I didn’t choose it.” He nodded toward the couch, the table, the arranged quiet of the place. “Most of this was already here when I moved in.”
Anri’s amazement shifted, recalibrating. The rooms were still nicer than she had expected. They just stopped looking like a carefully built nest and started looking like a life he had stepped into.
Curtis pointed with two fingers, more precise now.
“The books are mine. That shelf. The guitar. The computer. A few of the smaller things.” Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as his mismatched eyes moved over the pieces that actually belonged to him. “Those I brought. The rest is just… what the place already was.”
He did not add who had chosen the flat.
He did not add that the choice had been made for a we that no longer existed.
The distinction was enough: the home looked like him from a distance, and only certain objects were actually his when you got close.
Anri followed the line of the books, then the guitar, then the still-packed boxes that suddenly made more sense.
“…So the things that feel like you are the things you set down in a room somebody else already finished.” She said it softly, not as a probe. “That actually suits you more than if you’d decorated the whole place.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged, faint and tired.
“Yeah. I live around what’s already here. I just put my own stuff on top of it.”
In the cleaned evening light, with Anri standing in the new Nikes in a furnished flat he had not chosen, that sentence covered more of him than he intended — and still left the chooser’s name in the silence where the rest of the furniture had come from.
Curtis tipped his head toward the couch, red cap shifting as he stepped out of the host-stand in the hallway and into the room with her.
“Make yourself comfy.” A short pause, mismatched eyes on her now instead of the furniture. “Why the sudden urge to hang out tonight? I’m curious.”
Anri sat, then immediately looked like sitting had been the easy part. The new Nikes stayed close together on his floor. Her hands folded in her lap, then unfolded, then settled on her bag strap as if she needed something to hold that was not the real answer.
“…You said a lot has been happening. And that you didn’t want to be home alone.” That much was safe. True, even. She kept her eyes on the table a second longer than she needed to. “After the hallway, after I made you look like that, I didn’t want the rest of the day to end with you walking around by yourself until dinner, and then coming back to an empty place.”
A thinner breath.
“I know I asked late. I know it looked sudden.” Color touched her face. She did not mention Rio. She did not mention stay away from him. She did not mention the private satisfaction of getting the later hour first. “I just… didn’t want to leave you in that defeated face and call it space. If you were going to stay out, I wanted to be one of the reasons you weren’t doing it alone.”
She glanced up at him then, careful and earnest in the furnished room she had not expected.
“That’s the part I can say.”
The rest — Rio’s lecture, the claim, the fear of someone else arriving at this door first — stayed in her chest, sitting next to the new shoes and the fact that he had still let her in.
Curtis watched her for a second after she finished, red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes, one thumb hooked loosely in a white suspender like he needed somewhere to put the part she had not said.
“That’s a fair reason.”
He sat on the other end of the couch, not crowding her, long black braids shifting against the black shirt. The later hour settled between them without becoming an interrogation.
“You don’t have to tell me the rest if you’re not ready. I can hear there’s more in it than ‘you didn’t want me walking around alone.’” No accusation. Just the same quiet read he used on rooms. “But the part you can say is enough for me to let you in. After the hallway, I figured you’d want distance, not my address at night.”
A smaller pause. His mouth tugged, tired and almost wry.
“For the record… I didn’t look defeated because you asked me not to replace everything. I looked like that because I thought I’d made you feel like a problem I had to buy my way out of.” He glanced at the new Nikes on his floor, then back to her face. “So if you came because you didn’t want that to be the last version of the day, I get it. I’m glad you came.”
He leaned back into the furnished couch he had not chosen.
“You can stay as long as the late hour still feels okay. No lecture. No extra rescue. Just… hang out. Like you asked.”
Anri let out a breath she had been holding since the hallway.
“…Okay.” She glanced around the furnished room again, then at him in the red cap and suspenders, as if the cleaner look still needed getting used to up close. “You look different like this. Not bad. Just—less like school. Less like the person who ran out the gates with a box.”
Curtis huffed a quiet sound.
“That was the point. I wasn’t leaving the building. Figured I should look like I actually live in it.”
Anri’s eyes dropped to the Nikes, then away.
“They fit,” she said. “The shoes. I walked here in them.” A smaller pause. “I meant what I said about not making it a habit. I also meant what I said on the phone. Both of those can be true.”
“I know.” He tipped his head against the couch. “I’m not going to buy you a replacement life every time something breaks. I just didn’t want you walking home ruined because the old pair got wrecked.”
Silence held for a comfortable second. Then Anri, because the question had been sitting in her since third period:
“…Are you really not sleeping? You looked exhausted this morning, and then you still left school, bought these, went to dinner with doctors, and now you’re… this.” She gestured faintly at the tie. “How are you even upright?”
Curtis shrugged one shoulder.
“Insomnia’s old. Upright is just practice. I’ll pay for it tomorrow in class.” A dry flicker. “If I fall asleep at the desk again, don’t let them make me class-rep a second time by accident.”
That pulled a real, small laugh out of her. It surprised them both.
“Ryuugamine would notice,” she said, then caught the missing -kun too late and kept going anyway. “He already noticed enough today.”
Curtis did not pounce on the name change. He only watched her, mismatched eyes quieter.
“Yeah. He found the old pair. I didn’t plan that doorway. For what it’s worth.”
Anri nodded, fingers tight on her bag strap.
“I know you didn’t. That’s why Rio—” She stopped. Started again, thinner. “That’s why the hallway got so sharp. People saw it before I could put it in the right order.”
Curtis let the almost-name pass. Rio could wait. The flat did not need another triangle announced on the first night Anri had walked into it.
“You hungry?” he asked instead. “I can make tea. Or you can pick something and I’ll stop playing host.”
Anri looked at him, at the books that were actually his, at the rooms he had not chosen, and shook her head once.
“…Tea is fine. I didn’t come here to be entertained.” A softer beat. “I came because you said you didn’t want to be alone. That’s still the part I’m here for.”
Curtis stood, red cap shadowing his face as he headed for the kettle.
“Then that’s the part you get,” he said. “No extra story. No extra rescue. Just tea.”
Behind him, Anri sat in the furnished quiet with new shoes on her feet and the later hour finally looking like what she had claimed it was — not a prize over Rio, not a correction of Mikado, just a boy in a red cap making tea because she had asked to be in the room while he did not want to be home alone.
Anri wrapped her hands around the tea when he set it down, then glanced at him over the rim.
“…How was dinner with the doctors?”
Curtis grimaced.
The red cap tipped as the sushi counter came back in pieces he was willing to give: firecrackers, specimen eyes, a woman asking for his address, a question dressed up as science. None of that belonged on this couch.
“Well. It was dinner.” A short, dry pause. “I actually didn’t eat much. Shinra is interesting. His associate is… well. A free spirit who hides behind smart words to hide her intentions. Whatever they may be.”
Anri watched the grimace linger after the sentence ended. That told her more than the review.
“…That bad?” she asked, careful. “You said two doctors. I thought it would just be strange conversation and weird sushi.”
She turned the cup slowly.
“An associate hiding her intentions sounds like you spent the meal being studied.” Her eyes flicked over his cleaner evening look, then back to his face. “Is that why you didn’t eat? Or why you left to meet me instead of staying longer?”
A smaller beat, softer.
“You don’t have to tell me what she wanted. I can already hear that you didn’t like it.” Anri’s voice lowered. “Just — if dinner was that kind of room, I’m glad you didn’t stay in it until it got worse. Even if I only got the leftover hour.”
Curtis turned his own cup once, red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes as he looked at her instead of the leftover dinner.
“It wasn’t leftover.”
Anri’s fingers stilled on the tea.
“I was already going to leave that table,” he said. “Too many questions dressed up as manners. I didn’t come home because the food was bad. I came home because I told you after, and I meant after.” A short pause, plainer than the grimace. “If I wanted another hour of being studied, I would’ve stayed. I wanted this room. That’s why I changed instead of keeping the uniform on and wandering around until they got bored.”
He tipped his head toward the Nikes on his floor, then back to her face.
“So yeah. I’m glad you came. Not as the thing that happened after the doctors. As the reason I didn’t let the doctors keep the rest of the night.”
The happiness reached Anri before she could manage it into something smaller. It showed in her eyes first, then in the unguarded lift at the corner of her mouth, the later hour suddenly feeling claimed in the direction she had hoped and not only in the direction she had competed for.
“…Oh.” The word came out soft, almost embarrassed by how much it helped. “That’s—good. I mean. I’m glad. I didn’t want to be the leftover hour.”
She looked down at the tea, still smiling despite herself.
“Thank you for saying it like that, Curtis-kun.”
The nicer stretch of the evening lasted until the phone buzzed.
Curtis glanced down out of habit, red cap tipping forward. Rio’s name sat on the screen while Anri was still smiling into her tea two feet away, new Nikes on his floor, the not leftover sentence still warm in the room.
He opened it.
Rio:
you home?
Rio:
hallway was ugly. you looked wrecked when you left.
Rio:
i said i might come by tonight.
are you actually alone or did the whole day follow you home?
A last one landed while he was still reading.
Rio:
if you’re free i can come over.
you keep showing up for everyone else.
Anri saw the shift in his face before she saw the name. The happiness from a minute ago tightened at the edges.
“…Curtis-kun?”
He locked the screen too late for it to look like nothing. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as he set the phone face-down on the table, which only made it more obvious.
“Rio,” he said, because lying about the sender would be worse than the text itself.
Anri’s hands closed around the cup. The later hour she had claimed, the stay away from him she had muttered, the lecture in the hallway — all of it came back at once and sat down on the couch with them. Rio was not outside yet. She was on the glass. Asking if he was alone. Offering to come over. Saying he kept showing up for everyone else.
Which was exactly the sentence Anri had tried to outrun by getting here first.
The furnished flat, the cleaner look, the tea, the not leftover — all of it suddenly had a second knock waiting behind it, even if that knock was still only a message.
Curtis left the phone face-down and watched Anri instead of the screen. Red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes, he turned Rio’s last line over once like evidence that did not belong to this couch.
“‘You keep showing up for everybody else.’” A short pause. “That’s odd to say.”
Anri’s grip on the cup had already given her away. He nodded at it, not unkindly.
“And your body language shifted when I mentioned who texted me.” Long black braids moved against the black shirt as he leaned forward a fraction. “Did something happen after I left the campus today, Anri-chan?”
The furnished quiet went thin.
Anri stared into the tea like it might offer a version of the hallway that did not include Rio’s lecture, stay away from him, or the fact that she was sitting in his flat because she had gotten here first. Her mouth opened. Closed. The honest piece and the competitive piece were no longer separable enough to pick one.
“…Yes,” she said finally, small and reluctant. “Rio and I… talked. After you walked away.”
A thinner breath.
“She said I treat you like you should stop worrying about me. That I lectured you for doing something kind. That nobody is there for you, and you just keep being there for everyone else.” Anri’s eyes flicked to the face-down phone, then away. “She said she might come see you tonight.”
Color touched her face. The rest cost more.
“That’s part of why I asked where you were. Not the only part. I did mean I didn’t want you alone after that look on your face. But I also—” She swallowed. “I didn’t want her to be the one who got here first. I know how that sounds. I still came.”
The new Nikes stayed still on his floor. The later hour was no longer only tea and not leftover. It had a hallway argument sitting in it, and Rio’s text glowing under the table like the second half of a sentence Anri had tried to finish before it arrived.
Curtis paused.
He closed his eyes, red cap tipping forward as he set his hand underneath his chin like the hallway had just become a file he needed to sort in silence. Long black braids hung still against the black shirt. The face-down phone, Anri’s confession, Rio’s you keep showing up for everybody else — he let all of it sit without grabbing at any one piece first.
“…I see.” A short, low breath. “Hmm.”
That was all.
No immediate verdict. No joke to make the triangle smaller. No reassurance that made Anri’s I didn’t want her to get here first disappear, and no punishment for it either. Just the quiet of a man who had walked out of school defeated, sat through a dinner that tried to take his address, and come home to a nicer hour that was now revealing the race underneath it.
Anri watched his face and could not tell yet whether hmm meant disappointment, understanding, or the particular tiredness of someone realizing the day had followed him home after all.
The kettle’s leftover warmth and the furnished quiet held the sound of that single considering note, and nothing else, while Curtis kept his eyes closed and his hand under his chin as if the next sentence would have to be accurate enough to justify opening them.
Curtis shrugged, eyes opening, hand dropping from his chin as if the file had resolved into something too ordinary to keep posing over.
“Yeah. To be honest, I’ve been stressed and tired.” The red cap shadowed the mismatched eyes while he chose words that would not drag Anri into rooms she did not own. “Things I have been doing or saying have been met with looks and disdain.”
A short pause. He did not mention the van, or being called a problem while Kaztano was still the point. He did not mention Celty at his door demanding answers until the answers put blood on the floor. Those stayed in the pile without names.
“Whenever I try to help, it’s met with a lecture. Whenever I say something, it makes people uncomfortable.” His mouth tugged, tired more than bitter. “It can be quite exhausting at times. Especially when you’re just trying to help.”
Then the old seal, dropped on top like a lid that no longer fit.
“…But it’s fine. I’m fine.”
Anri set the tea down harder than she meant to.
“No.” The word came out immediate, then she pulled it back into something she could keep in his living room. “Don’t finish it like that. You just told me people look at you with disdain when you help, and that talking makes them uncomfortable, and that it’s exhausting — and then you put I’m fine on it like that cancels the rest.”
Her hands tightened in her lap.
“That hallway was part of what you mean. I know it was. I lectured you after you ran out and bought me shoes. Rio said I make you feel alienated, and I couldn’t argue because she was right about the way it landed.” A thinner breath. “So if helping keeps getting you looks and speeches, I am one of the people who did that to you today. You don’t get to sit there and call the whole pile fine just to keep this hour from getting heavier.”
She searched his face under the red cap, less angry than unwilling to let him swallow it.
“You can be tired. You can be stressed. You can even be sick of being the person who shows up. What you don’t have to do is tell me you’re fine when you just described the opposite.” A quieter beat. “I’m already here, Curtis-kun. You don’t have to tidy the truth for me.”
Curtis leaned back into the couch, red cap tipping as a thin laugh escaped him before the next piece did.
“And yesterday. When I took the guy down for calling you such a derogatory name. I felt like a freak again. Ahaha.”
The laugh did not hide it. It just gave the word somewhere to sit that was not complete silence. His mismatched eyes flicked toward the window, then away.
“Well. At least Shizuo knows how that feels. Wonder if he would be open to hang out with me.” Another dry little huff. “Nah. Probably a bad idea. We would end up destroying the city. Ahaha.”
Anri did not laugh with the second joke.
The first one had already put her back on the edge of that street: the insult, the knee, the slam, the crack, the crowd cheering a myth while she stared at him like the boy from the café had stepped out of category. Freak was the word he had used when he walked away from her. Hearing it again in his own living room, attached to the thing he had done in her defense, made the tea go tasteless.
“…Curtis-kun.” Her voice came out quiet and tight. “You don’t have to turn that into a bit. I was there. I know why you did it. I also know I looked at you wrong after, and that look is part of why you disappeared.”
A shorter beat.
“Shizuo is not the only person who can sit with that feeling. And you and him flattening the city is not the only alternative to saying you felt like a freak.” She held his gaze under the red cap. “You protected me. The stare was my failure. If you’re still carrying the word, don’t put a laugh on it just so I can pretend yesterday ended cleaner than it did.”
Curtis shook his head once, red cap tipping as he let the laugh die for good.
“Nah. It’s fine, Anri-chan. I just gotta accept I might not be accepted if I show who I truly am.” A short pause, mismatched eyes on the table instead of her. “People demand a certain version of me. Gentle and kind. Which is me. But the moment I show the other half, it’s like the other version never existed. So since I’ve been here… no one’s been actually fully comfortable with the way I truly am.”
Anri’s voice came out thinner than she wanted.
“…And Rio?”
Curtis did not hesitate long enough to make the answer kinder.
“Rio comes over to chat and vent. She also listens to my problems. However, she doesn’t stare at me like I caused the problem rather than I simply solved them.”
Anri’s hands tightened around each other in her lap.
The new Nikes stayed still on his floor. The later hour, the not leftover, the tea — all of it narrowed to that one distinction he had just drawn without even trying to cut her with it. Rio looked at the solution. Anri had looked at the violence. Rio got to be the person he could sit with after. Anri had gotten the defeated walk-away and a lecture she could not refute.
In her mind the hallway mutter came back, sharper now that it had a reason sitting on his couch.
She is going to stay away from you.
Out loud, she kept it from becoming that sentence.
“…So that’s the difference.” Her voice was careful, almost too even. “She sees the help. I made you feel like the problem. That’s what you’re saying.”
A short breath. Her fingers would not loosen.
“I already know I stared wrong. I already know Rio got that part right in the hallway. You don’t have to use her as the example of someone who can stand the whole of you unless you want me to sit here measuring myself against that.” Her eyes flicked to the face-down phone, then back to him. “I’m trying to be here for you. If the version of me that got here first still looks at you like a fracture, then tell me that plainly. Don’t make me hear it through how comfortable Rio is.”
The thought stayed anyway, hot and unkind under the composed words:
Stay away from him.
Curtis saw her hands before he finished the comparison. Red cap tipping, he eased the next part down instead of driving it in.
“That’s the thing she doesn’t understand.” A short pause. “If she saw what you saw… I know those conversations wouldn’t have happened.”
The distinction shifted. Rio’s comfort was not automatically proof she could hold the whole of him. It was proof she had not been standing at the edge of that street when the other half came out and the pavement answered.
His gaze dropped to Anri’s fists.
“And Anri-chan — unclench your fists. No one is here to hurt you.”
The furnished room went very still around that.
Anri looked down, startled to find her own hands locked white in her lap like she was still in the hallway waiting for a fight. She forced them open, fingers stiff, then curled them loosely again because fully empty hands felt worse.
“…I’m not scared you’re going to hurt me.” The words came out quiet and tangled. “That’s not why.”
A thinner breath.
“If Rio didn’t see what I saw, then of course she can sit here and listen and not look at you like a fracture. I saw the other half. I stared. I made you leave. And now I’m in your house listening to you explain that the girl who didn’t watch it happen gets the easier conversations.” Her voice frayed, then steadied. “You telling me to unclench doesn’t make that smaller. It just reminds me I’m sitting here ready for a blow that isn’t even aimed at my body.”
She left her hands open on her knees, visibly working to keep them that way.
“I’m trying, Curtis-kun. I came here trying. Don’t look at my fists and think I’m expecting you to become the street again. I’m expecting to lose the place I just got, and that is a different kind of flinch.”
Curtis shook his head once, red cap tipping as he caught the flinch under her open hands and went for the true piece instead of another comparison.
“You’re not going to lose a place in me.” The words came out plain, no joke on them. “I just hated being looked at the way I did by the person who I…”
He stopped.
Anri looked at him.
The unfinished line sat between them with nowhere to hide: the person who I— and then nothing. Not Rio. Not the crowd. Her. The stare after the fight. The person who I—care about, trust, want near, cannot stand looking at me like a freak. He did not pick the verb. He left it on the edge of his mouth and let the silence finish the danger of it.
Then Curtis smiled.
It was small, almost gentle, and absolutely a door closing on the rest of the sentence. He lifted his coffee, drank, and used the cup like a period.
Anri stayed frozen in that look, new Nikes still on his floor, fists unclenched only because he had asked. The later hour had just tried to become a confession and then turned into a sip of coffee instead. Whatever place he had just promised she would not lose was now sitting right next to the word he would not say.
“…Curtis-kun.” His name came out barely above the steam. “The person who you what?”
He kept the smile. He kept the cup. He did not finish the sentence.
Curtis chuckled into the cup, red cap tipping as he leaned back like the danger of the unfinished line had been a game he was now choosing not to lose.
“Nope! I’m not telling you~”
The tease was light on purpose. Too light for the sentence he had almost walked into, which was the point. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as he set the coffee down, mismatched eyes amused in a way that dared her to keep pulling at a door he had just locked with a sing-song refusal.
Anri stared at him, color rising, the person who I— still sitting in the air between them like a shoe he had picked up and then refused to hand over.
“…That’s not fair.” It came out before she could make it sound composed. “You don’t get to start that and then nope at me.”
A thinner breath, frustrated and unwillingly warmed by the tease at the same time.
“Curtis-kun. You just told me I won’t lose a place in you, and then you almost named what that place is, and now you’re humming about not telling me.” Her hands stayed open in her lap only by force. “You can keep the rest. Fine. But don’t act like that was nothing. I heard where it was going.”
Curtis only smiled wider, unhelpful on purpose, and reached for the coffee again as if the later hour could survive on teasing where it had nearly tripped into a confession.
The tease did not hold.
Color crept up Curtis’s face under the red cap, mismatched eyes flicking away from her and then back like the lock on the sentence had failed. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as he muttered it instead of singing it.
“…C’mon, Anri-chan. Do I really gotta say it…”
It was not a no anymore. It was the sound of a boy who had already said enough — you’re not going to lose a place in me / the person who I— — and was now asking whether the last word still needed to be dragged into the open when it was sitting on the couch with them in every other shape.
Anri’s breath caught.
The blush did more damage than the unfinished line. He could shut Mei down like the ninth circle. He could walk out of school looking defeated. He could joke about flattening the city with Shizuo. And still this — do I really gotta say it — made him look suddenly younger and cornered by his own mouth.
“…If you don’t say it, I’m going to keep filling it in,” she got out, quieter than the protest before. “And I don’t want to fill it in wrong.”
A smaller beat. Her hands stayed open.
“You don’t have to make a speech. Just—don’t ask me to pretend I didn’t hear it. Please.”
The furnished room held the blush, the red cap, and the last unspoken piece of the person who I… while Anri waited to see whether he would actually say it, or whether do I really gotta was as close as the later hour would get.
Curtis stared at the coffee like it might offer him Justice’s vocabulary and then accepted that it would not. Red cap tipping, color still in his face, he forced the words through the only way he had them.
“I’m not my brother Justice. He could recite a lot of things to express himself.” A rough little breath. “For me… Anri-chan…”
His mismatched eyes lifted to her and stayed there, no tease left to hide behind.
“…I-I like you. A lot.”
The furnished flat went very quiet around it.
Not a poem. Not a speech. Not the kind of line Justice would have dressed in something prettier and more dangerous. Just the abandoned-cabin version of a confession: blunt, late, and so unprotected it made the earlier nope look like panic instead of play.
Anri sat frozen on his couch, new Nikes on the floor, tea forgotten, the hallway and Rio’s text and I can’t ignore you all colliding into the one sentence she had been trying not to invent for him. Her mouth opened. Nothing came out at first. Then her eyes shone in that helpless way that was not quite tears and not quite composure.
“…Curtis-kun.”
His name left her like she needed to make sure the person who had said it was still the same boy in the red cap. A shaky breath followed.
“You—” She stopped, then the rest came out smaller and unmistakably happy under the shock. “You like me.”
It was not a question. It was her repeating it so the room would have to keep it.
She did not lunge. She did not announce a future. She only looked at him as if the defeated face from the hallway and this blushing, stumbling I like you a lot had finally become the same person, and the later hour had been waiting for exactly that and no other prize.
“…I heard you,” she whispered. “You don’t have to recite anything else.”
Her hands were still open in her lap. This time they were not clenched against a loss. They were just trying to hold the fact that he had said it.
